1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,411
We ran the MMD metric
from Josh's package on some toy cells,

2
00:00:04,411 --> 00:00:06,885
and it said zero point three two six.

3
00:00:07,205 --> 00:00:08,317
Is that right?

4
00:00:08,367 --> 00:00:12,428
You can't tell by looking.
Nobody can check a number like that by eye.

5
00:00:12,428 --> 00:00:16,431
But you can check a metric on data
where you already know the answer.

6
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:19,971
That's all a test is:
a question with a known answer,

7
00:00:19,971 --> 00:00:23,405
which your code answers by itself,
every time you ask.

8
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:29,927
Josh already wrote one, in test_evaluation.py.

9
00:00:30,247 --> 00:00:32,047
Three cells, two genes.

10
00:00:32,167 --> 00:00:34,791
The prediction and the truth are the same cloud.

11
00:00:34,967 --> 00:00:40,317
MMD compares two clouds with a kernel.
Add the average similarity inside each cloud,

12
00:00:40,317 --> 00:00:43,820
then subtract twice the average
similarity across them.

13
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,898
When the clouds are identical,

14
00:00:45,898 --> 00:00:49,504
all three averages are equal,
so the answer must be zero.

15
00:00:49,667 --> 00:00:54,835
The last line says exactly that:
assert that the score is approximately zero.

16
00:00:54,835 --> 00:01:00,071
Approximately, because sums of floating-point
numbers are rarely exactly zero.

17
00:01:00,071 --> 00:01:05,709
If it is, the test passes, silently.
If not, it fails, loudly, showing both numbers.

18
00:01:09,633 --> 00:01:15,959
You never call a test yourself. You type pytest.
It finds files named test underscore something,

19
00:01:15,959 --> 00:01:20,769
runs every function whose name starts with test,
and reports on each one.

20
00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:24,919
On its own,
Josh's MMD test passes in under two seconds,

21
00:01:24,919 --> 00:01:28,122
and most of that is just importing libraries.

22
00:01:28,442 --> 00:01:31,898
Pause and predict:
what happens when you run the whole folder?

23
00:01:36,066 --> 00:01:40,623
It stops early. test_model.py
isn't a test function, it's a script.

24
00:01:40,623 --> 00:01:46,405
Its code runs the moment pytest imports it.
Off the cluster it can't find its config.

25
00:01:46,405 --> 00:01:48,173
On the cluster it's worse:

26
00:01:48,173 --> 00:01:50,923
it loads the multi-gigabyte atlas files,

27
00:01:50,923 --> 00:01:54,498
then crashes in setup,
before a single test has run.

28
00:01:54,666 --> 00:01:59,661
A good test runs on your code alone.
No cluster, no gigabyte files, no GPU.

29
00:01:59,661 --> 00:02:03,456
That's what makes it fast enough
to run after every edit.

30
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:12,305
Where do known answers come from?
From what the metric means.

31
00:02:12,305 --> 00:02:15,868
Pause and predict:
push one cloud away from the other.

32
00:02:15,868 --> 00:02:17,668
What should MMD do?

33
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:23,100
It should grow, and it does.

34
00:02:23,156 --> 00:02:28,593
We measured it: shift by nothing, half, one,
then two, and MMD climbs from zero,

35
00:02:28,593 --> 00:02:32,670
to point zero eight,
to point two nine, to point eight five.

36
00:02:32,833 --> 00:02:36,502
A perfect prediction should score
an R squared of one, and it does.

37
00:02:36,822 --> 00:02:40,292
Shuffle the predicted cells,
and it drops to almost zero,

38
00:02:40,292 --> 00:02:43,457
because this R squared pairs
cell one with cell one.

39
00:02:43,777 --> 00:02:48,569
Remember that. Each of those sentences
becomes a test of about five lines.

40
00:02:48,889 --> 00:02:52,367
And notice the data:
tiny arrays built inside the test.

41
00:02:52,687 --> 00:02:55,609
The test checks the logic, not the biology.

42
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:02,755
Now a test that does more than check arithmetic.

43
00:03:03,075 --> 00:03:04,257
In a real experiment,

44
00:03:04,257 --> 00:03:07,747
the mouse and human test sets
have different numbers of cells.

45
00:03:08,067 --> 00:03:11,736
Pause and predict:
does R2Evaluation accept that?

46
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:20,447
It doesn't. We tried five mouse
cells against seven human cells,

47
00:03:20,447 --> 00:03:25,421
and it crashed at line 313.
Five rows can't be lined up against seven.

48
00:03:25,421 --> 00:03:29,116
And with five against five,
it doesn't crash at all:

49
00:03:29,116 --> 00:03:34,375
it pairs unrelated cells and reports
an R squared of point one zero three.

50
00:03:34,534 --> 00:03:36,390
The reason is in its formula.

51
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:40,822
It's the same per-gene R squared
as in your reconstruction script,

52
00:03:40,822 --> 00:03:44,561
where every cell has a true partner:
its own reconstruction.

53
00:03:44,881 --> 00:03:50,470
Your benchmark uses a different R squared:
it compares the average expression of each gene.

54
00:03:50,790 --> 00:03:53,840
An R squared of means
wouldn't even notice a shuffle.

55
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,325
So, which one does the experiment need?

56
00:03:56,645 --> 00:03:58,409
The test can't answer that.

57
00:03:58,459 --> 00:04:02,875
It turned a hidden assumption
into a decision you can now make on purpose.

58
00:04:08,333 --> 00:04:09,883
Tests come in layers.

59
00:04:09,933 --> 00:04:14,264
At the bottom are unit tests:
one class, toy arrays, milliseconds.

60
00:04:14,584 --> 00:04:15,916
Write many of these.

61
00:04:15,966 --> 00:04:22,130
In the middle, the whole Experiment runs
on a few tiny h5ad files, from a toy config.

62
00:04:22,130 --> 00:04:24,233
Seconds. A handful is enough.

63
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:27,831
Josh's toy_config.yaml
is the seed of this layer,

64
00:04:27,831 --> 00:04:29,961
once it's brought up to date.

65
00:04:30,133 --> 00:04:34,146
At the top is the e24_replica
notebook on the real atlas:

66
00:04:34,146 --> 00:04:36,469
minutes to hours, on the cluster.

67
00:04:36,789 --> 00:04:40,138
Run it once the layers below are green,
not instead of them.

68
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:43,999
The working loop:
change one thing, run pytest, see green,

69
00:04:43,999 --> 00:04:46,422
and only then go back to the notebook.

70
00:04:50,267 --> 00:04:54,579
Recap. Nobody can check zero
point three two six by eye,

71
00:04:54,579 --> 00:04:59,910
but you can check that the code gives zero,
one, and more, exactly where it must.

72
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:02,827
A test is a question with a known answer,

73
00:05:02,827 --> 00:05:06,566
and pytest runs every function
whose name starts with test.

74
00:05:06,734 --> 00:05:10,604
Known answers come from meaning:
identical clouds give zero,

75
00:05:10,604 --> 00:05:14,926
a perfect prediction gives one,
and more shift gives more distance.

76
00:05:15,246 --> 00:05:19,961
A test can also expose an assumption:
R2Evaluation pairs rows.

77
00:05:20,281 --> 00:05:23,672
And test in layers:
unit tests first, the notebook last.

78
00:05:23,834 --> 00:05:27,343
Your exercises:
turn each known answer into a test,

79
00:05:27,343 --> 00:05:31,816
move test_model's code
into a test function that uses tiny files,

80
00:05:31,816 --> 00:05:35,119
and decide which R squared the experiment means.
